WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.313
In part one we found the gcd

2
00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:08.070
of eighty-four and thirty, six

3
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.880
by listing divisors.

4
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.517
Now a fast method.

5
00:00:11.603 --> 00:00:13.585
Cut the largest squares

6
00:00:13.671 --> 00:00:15.567
you can off the board

7
00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:17.980
two squares of side thirty.

8
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.381
What is left is a piece

9
00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.431
twenty-four by thirty.

10
00:00:22.431 --> 00:00:25.374
Eighty-four is two times thirty

11
00:00:25.374 --> 00:00:26.979
plus twenty-four.

12
00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:30.189
Does the piece have the same common

13
00:00:30.278 --> 00:00:32.774
divisors as the whole board?

14
00:00:32.774 --> 00:00:34.290
Pause and think.

15
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:38.295
Yes.

16
00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:42.229
Let e divide eighty-four and thirty.

17
00:00:42.335 --> 00:00:45.099
Then e divides eighty-four

18
00:00:45.206 --> 00:00:47.651
minus two times thirty

19
00:00:47.651 --> 00:00:49.990
which is twenty-four.

20
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.126
Now the other way.

21
00:00:52.126 --> 00:00:55.797
Let e divide thirty and twenty-four.

22
00:00:55.896 --> 00:00:58.972
Then e divides two times thirty

23
00:00:59.071 --> 00:01:02.940
plus twenty-four, which is eighty-four.

24
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.208
So the two pairs have exactly

25
00:01:06.308 --> 00:01:08.823
the same common divisors

26
00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:11.540
and the same greatest one.

27
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.162
In general: if a equals q b plus r

28
00:01:15.262 --> 00:01:17.576
then the gcd of a and b

29
00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.890
is the gcd of b and r.

30
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.925
To prove two sets are equal

31
00:01:22.925 --> 00:01:26.760
we showed each one is inside the other.

32
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:27.982
Repeat.

33
00:01:27.982 --> 00:01:31.470
From the twenty-four by thirty piece

34
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.774
cut one square of side twenty-four.

35
00:01:34.774 --> 00:01:38.721
Thirty is one times twenty-four, plus six.

36
00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:42.300
Left over: a strip twenty-four by six.

37
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.109
That strip is exactly four

38
00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:47.095
squares of side six

39
00:01:47.095 --> 00:01:51.067
twenty-four is four times six, plus zero.

40
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:53.810
Nothing is left, so we stop.

41
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.237
By the lemma

42
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.304
the gcd of eighty-four and thirty is

43
00:01:58.387 --> 00:02:01.204
the gcd of thirty and twenty-four

44
00:02:01.204 --> 00:02:04.602
which is the gcd of twenty-four and six

45
00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:05.762
which is six.

46
00:02:05.845 --> 00:02:08.165
And squares of side six tile

47
00:02:08.248 --> 00:02:10.320
every piece of the board.

48
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.600
Why does this always stop?

49
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.962
The remainders strictly decrease: thirty

50
00:02:17.962 --> 00:02:22.013
then twenty-four, then six, then zero.

51
00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:26.271
Natural numbers cannot decrease forever

52
00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:28.140
by well-ordering.

53
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.019
And the gcd never changes from

54
00:02:31.104 --> 00:02:32.874
one line to the next.

55
00:02:32.874 --> 00:02:35.909
So when the remainder reaches zero

56
00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:39.450
the answer is the last nonzero remainder.

57
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.501
Not the last remainder, zero

58
00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:45.575
and not the last quotient, four.

59
00:02:45.575 --> 00:02:46.600
It is six.

60
00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.252
It is fast, too.

61
00:02:48.252 --> 00:02:51.262
Ten thousand four hundred and three

62
00:02:51.344 --> 00:02:53.785
and nine thousand nine hundred

63
00:02:53.866 --> 00:02:56.470
and ninety-one: three divisions

64
00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.561
and the gcd is one hundred and three.

65
00:02:59.561 --> 00:03:01.270
No factoring needed.

66
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.655
Now run the algorithm backwards.

67
00:03:04.743 --> 00:03:07.724
The second line says six is thirty

68
00:03:07.811 --> 00:03:10.266
minus one times twenty-four.

69
00:03:10.354 --> 00:03:14.386
The first line says twenty-four is eighty-four

70
00:03:14.474 --> 00:03:16.490
minus two times thirty.

71
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.518
Put that in for twenty-four.

72
00:03:19.518 --> 00:03:21.623
Collect the thirties

73
00:03:21.623 --> 00:03:25.449
six equals eighty-four times minus one

74
00:03:25.449 --> 00:03:27.840
plus thirty times three.

75
00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.453
That is a recipe with rods.

76
00:03:30.453 --> 00:03:34.225
Lay three thirty-centimetre rods end to end

77
00:03:34.225 --> 00:03:34.896
ninety.

78
00:03:34.896 --> 00:03:36.069
Next to them

79
00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:38.752
one eighty-four-centimetre rod.

80
00:03:38.752 --> 00:03:41.350
The difference is exactly six.

81
00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.926
This always works.

82
00:03:43.926 --> 00:03:46.032
Bézout's identity

83
00:03:46.032 --> 00:03:49.026
for a and b not both zero

84
00:03:49.136 --> 00:03:52.573
there are integers x and y with

85
00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:56.010
the gcd equal to a x plus b y.

86
00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:56.951
Proof.

87
00:03:57.049 --> 00:04:00.692
Let d be the smallest positive number

88
00:04:00.791 --> 00:04:03.252
of the form a x plus b y

89
00:04:03.252 --> 00:04:05.615
well-ordering gives it.

90
00:04:05.615 --> 00:04:07.092
Divide a by d.

91
00:04:07.191 --> 00:04:09.554
The remainder is also of

92
00:04:09.652 --> 00:04:13.788
the form a x plus b y, and smaller than d

93
00:04:13.788 --> 00:04:15.757
so it must be zero.

94
00:04:15.757 --> 00:04:19.400
So d divides a, and in the same way

95
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.680
d divides b.

96
00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.366
And any common divisor of a and

97
00:04:24.474 --> 00:04:28.240
b divides a x plus b y, which is d.

98
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.069
So d is the gcd.

99
00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:33.082
The x and y are not unique

100
00:04:33.082 --> 00:04:35.665
eighty-four times four

101
00:04:35.665 --> 00:04:39.108
plus thirty times minus eleven

102
00:04:39.108 --> 00:04:40.830
is six as well.

103
00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.430
A warning.

104
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:44.777
Eighty-four times two

105
00:04:44.777 --> 00:04:49.163
plus thirty times minus five, is eighteen.

106
00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:52.530
Does that make eighteen the gcd?

107
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:53.166
No.

108
00:04:53.166 --> 00:04:57.266
Eighteen does not even divide eighty-four.

109
00:04:57.361 --> 00:04:59.840
Bézout says the gcd can be

110
00:04:59.935 --> 00:05:02.223
written as a x plus b y.

111
00:05:02.318 --> 00:05:04.797
It does not say that every

112
00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:07.181
a x plus b y is the gcd.

113
00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:11.280
All we learn is that six divides eighteen.

114
00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.049
Bézout also proves what the summary

115
00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.873
listed next to the definition

116
00:05:17.873 --> 00:05:21.336
every common divisor divides the gcd.

117
00:05:21.336 --> 00:05:24.980
One, two, three and six all divide six.

118
00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.258
Two numbers are relatively prime

119
00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:30.362
when their gcd is one.

120
00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:33.564
That happens exactly when a x plus

121
00:05:33.655 --> 00:05:36.400
b y equals one has a solution.

122
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.778
Thirty-five and eighteen

123
00:05:38.778 --> 00:05:41.431
thirty-five times minus one

124
00:05:41.431 --> 00:05:44.450
plus eighteen times two, is one.

125
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.235
That one line is a certificate

126
00:05:47.317 --> 00:05:48.697
anyone can check.

127
00:05:48.778 --> 00:05:51.132
But ninety-one and sixty-five

128
00:05:51.213 --> 00:05:53.324
share the factor thirteen

129
00:05:53.324 --> 00:05:55.840
they are not relatively prime.

130
00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.256
So: to find the gcd, divide

131
00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:03.815
then replace a and b by b and the remainder

132
00:06:03.815 --> 00:06:06.753
until the remainder is zero.

133
00:06:06.753 --> 00:06:08.576
Run it backwards

134
00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:12.020
and the gcd becomes a x plus b y.

135
00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.728
Try it yourself

136
00:06:13.813 --> 00:06:17.208
find the gcd of one thousand two hundred

137
00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:20.264
sixty and one hundred ninety-eight

138
00:06:20.264 --> 00:06:22.810
and write it as a x plus b y.

139
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.152
Next lecture leaves numbers for graphs

140
00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.760
dots, and the lines between them.
